staramp links FFmpeg (https://ffmpeg.org) -- libavformat, libavcodec,
libavutil and libswresample -- to decode the formats symphonia does not.
FFmpeg is the FFmpeg project's work, not staramp's.

Most of FFmpeg is licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License,
version 2.1 or later; its text is in LICENSES/ffmpeg-LGPL-2.1.txt. Some
builds are configured with `--enable-gpl` or `--enable-version3`, which put
the resulting libraries under the GPL or under version 3 of either licence,
and a binary linked against such a build inherits those terms. FFmpeg's own
LICENSE.md sets out which components fall where.

The libraries are always linked dynamically, never statically. The `.deb`,
the Arch package and the Nix package declare a dependency on the system
FFmpeg and use whatever that system provides. The AppImage and the portable
tarball carry a copy alongside the binary, because libavcodec's soname
differs on every distribution -- there the copy is Debian's build, it sits
beside the executable in `usr/lib`, and it can be replaced with another of
the same soname without rebuilding staramp.

Source for FFmpeg is available from the project at
https://ffmpeg.org/download.html, and for the build in a given package from
whichever distribution supplied it -- Debian's for the AppImage and the
tarball.

The visualizer is staramp's own. `src/vis/spectrum.rs` spaces its bands on
the ERB scale of Glasberg and Moore (1990) and weights them with the A curve
of IEC 61672-1, and `src/vis/meter.rs` follows the peak-programme meter
ballistics of IEC 60268-10. Those are published standards and formulae, not
anyone's source. Earlier releases carried analysis ported from cliamp and
cava; none of it remains.

The ten equalizer centre frequencies are Winamp's classic layout, which is a
specification rather than code. The filters are staramp's own.

The terminal text effects follow the designs of TerminalTextEffects
(https://github.com/ChrisBuilds/terminaltexteffects) and its Rust port ttfx
(https://github.com/omacom/ttfx). They are reimplemented here rather than
depended on -- those render ANSI to stdout, while staramp needs per-character
state it can draw into a ratatui buffer -- so no ttfx code is included.

Colour schemes under themes/ are transcriptions of published palettes:
Catppuccin, Dracula, Nord, Rose Pine, Tokyo Night, Gruvbox, Everforest,
Kanagawa, Ayu, One Dark and Solarized, each MIT-licensed or public. Each theme
file names its author.

themes/winamp-classic.toml transcribes the Winamp 2.91 base skin's own
PLEDIT.TXT and VISCOLOR.TXT values. staramp is not affiliated with, endorsed
by, or connected to Winamp, Nullsoft or the Llama Group.
